{"id":"17141b33-ff96-4e71-b65b-9c0c69f1c9e1","name":"Optimizing DataStateUseCase Discussion","timestamp":1742263331069,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"<user_query>
Optimize DataStateUseCase
</user_query>"},{"role":"assistant","content":"# DataStateUseCase Optimization

The current implementation is already quite concise and follows good practices, but here are some optimizations I can suggest:

```kotlin:app/src/main/java/tungp/android/bazarbooks/domain/usecase/DataStateUseCase.kt
abstract class DataStateUseCase<in Params, ReturnType> where ReturnType : Any {

 protected abstract suspend fun FlowCollector<BazaResult<ReturnType>>.execute(params: Params)

 // Allow for dispatcher injection to improve testability
 operator fun invoke(params: Params, dispatcher: kotlinx.coroutines.CoroutineDispatcher = Dispatchers.IO) = flow {
 execute(params)
 }
 .flowOn(dispatcher)
}
```

Key improvements:
1. Made the `invoke` function non-suspending since it's returning a Flow which is inherently asynchronous
2. Added optional dispatcher parameter with a default value of Dispatchers.IO
 - This improves testability by allowing you to inject Dispatchers.Unconfined during tests
 - Follows dependency injection principles by not hard-coding the dispatcher

These changes maintain the same functionality while making the class more flexible and easier to test."}]}

